<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Portuguese Guinea</h1><p class="st-meta">1588 CE – 1974 CE · capital Bissau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Portugal's colony on the Upper Guinea coast, which won independence as Guinea-Bissau after a liberation war led by Amilcar Cabral.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Portuguese and Cape Verdean traders had been active in the rivers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> coast since the 15th century. In 1588 the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacheu">Cacheu</a> was founded as a fortified trading base, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bissau">Bissau</a> in the late 17th century. For long the posts were governed from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-cape-verde">Portuguese Cape Verde</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>For centuries Portuguese control barely reached beyond a few trading towns, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luso-africans">Luso-African</a> traders dealt in slaves and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundnut">groundnuts</a>. The colony was separated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-verde">Cape Verde</a> in 1879, and military campaigns between 1912 and 1915 imposed control over the interior. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo</a> it was governed through chiefs and forced labour.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From 1963 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paigc">PAIGC</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amilcar-cabral">Amilcar Cabral</a> until his assassination in 1973, fought a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war that liberated much of the countryside. Independence was declared in 1973, and Portugal recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea-bissau">Guinea-Bissau</a> in 1974 after its revolution.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/portuguese-guinea.jpg" alt="Portuguese Guinea" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Columbano Bordalo Pinheiro Vector: Vítor Luís Rodrigues, António Martins-Tuválkin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Portugal_(official).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1588 CE – 1974 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bissau</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Honorio Pereira Barreto, Antonio de Spinola</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, Islam and traditional religions</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese and Guinea-Bissau Creole</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/guinea-bissau">Guinea-Bissau</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Guine Portuguesa, Portuguese Guinea colony</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1781&amp;focus=portuguese-guinea" aria-label="Portuguese Guinea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4252 1735 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Portuguese Guinea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4572 1991L4560 1941M4572 1991L4773 1044M4572 1991L4358 1879"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4560 1941h0M4773 1044h0M4358 1879h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4572 1991h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4572 1991h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bissau. <a href="/atlas?year=1781&amp;focus=portuguese-guinea">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
